@meta
id: 02-11
title: U puts the whole line back
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: undo
par: 84
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+Z, several times, carefully
open: notes.md

@brief
`u` walks back one change at a time. Sometimes you do not want to count —
you have fiddled with one line four or five times and you want it back the
way it was. Capital `U` does that in a single key: undo every change made
to the last line you changed, in one go.

It only works while you stay on that line. Touch another line and the
window closes.

The payments notes need one sentence added. But first, mess a line up.
In order:

  1. On the line about refunds, append ` (maybe)`. `Esc`.

  2. Still on that line, append ` (check)`. `Esc`.

  3. Still on that line, append ` (ask finance)`. `Esc`.

  4. All three were thinking out loud. Put the line back with one key.

  5. Now do the real work: append ` See the finance runbook.` to the
     chargebacks line, and save.

The refunds line must end up exactly as it started.

@keys
| `U` | undo all recent changes on the last changed line |
| `u` | undo one change |
| `Ctrl-R` | redo |

@hints
Three separate insert sessions on one line would take three `u` presses.
`U` is one press for the same result, as long as you have not moved off
the line.
--
`U` is capital. `u` and `U` are different commands, which is true of most
letter pairs in vim.
--
After `U`, move down one line with `j` and do the append there. Do not
press `U` again — a second `U` undoes the undo.

@solution
    jj $ a  (maybe) <Esc> $ a  (check) <Esc> $ a  (ask finance) <Esc>
    U
    j $ a  See the finance runbook. <Esc>
    ZZ

`U` is odd in a way worth knowing. It is itself a change, so `u` undoes
it and gives you your mess back, and pressing `U` twice toggles between
the two versions of the line. It also has no memory beyond one line: as
soon as you edit somewhere else, the line you were fiddling with is
frozen into the normal history and `U` will target the new line instead.

In practice `U` earns its keep in exactly the situation above — retyping
a value three times to see which reads best, then wanting the original.
The rest of the time `u` with a count is more predictable, because it
counts changes rather than guessing at your intent.

@start notes.md
# Payments notes

Refunds settle in 3 to 5 working days.
Chargebacks are handled by finance.

@expected notes.md
# Payments notes

Refunds settle in 3 to 5 working days.
Chargebacks are handled by finance. See the finance runbook.
